 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
   FOUR CRITICAL PITFALLS (each causes a hard crash)
 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

        P1  rel32 jump overflow
            Host EXE and injected DLL were 9 GB apart (0x223B4D140), far
            beyond the +-2 GB rel32 range. Truncation wrapped the target
            into a non-executable page -> 0xC0000005 EXECUTE violation.
            FIX: 14-byte absolute jump (FF 25 00 00 00 00 + imm64).

        P2  lost constructor return value (rax)
            MSVC x64 returns `this` in rax; the host factory function does
            a bare `ret` and hands rax to its caller. A `void` hook left
            rax as garbage -> wild pointer -> delayed heap corruption that
            surfaced inside Qt5Widgets layout code.
            FIX: the hook must `return self`.

        P3  missing third argument (r8)
            The factory sets r8 before calling the constructor, and the
            constructor forwards rcx/rdx/r8 straight into the base-class
            constructor. Dropping r8 fed it a garbage pointer.
            FIX: three-parameter hook signature.

        P4  trampoline stack semantics
            Entering a trampoline via `call` pushes an extra 8 bytes, so
            the original prologue wrote its shadow space into the hook's
            stack frame.
            FIX: unhook-call-rehook instead of a trampoline.

